WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.670
- Ernesto, thank you for inviting me.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.543
Thank you for gathering us.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.423
Nuclear arms control is a global service,

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:25.040
designed to protect the global commons.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.230
We can't protect the global commons,

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.123
if there are mushroom clouds.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.620
There has not been a single mushroom cloud

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.130
on a battlefield

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.543
since 1945.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.623
That's a remarkable achievement.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.053
Really an unheralded achievement.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.723
Nobody in 1945 who was
concerned about this problem,

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.123
dared to hope,

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.640
that we could go seven decades

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:08.360
without the battlefield
used of a nuclear weapon.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:09.193
Even more,

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.783
no major power, no regional power,

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.080
has even tested a nuclear weapon

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.893
underground, for two decades.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.373
This too is an extraordinary
accomplishment.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.573
A hard won accomplishment.

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:35.740
Because this treaty that
bans all nuclear tests

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.140
in all environments, has been a goal

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.930
for really since the
Kennedy administration,

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:49.003
actually make that the
Eisenhower administration.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.770
Finally arrived in 1996,

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.073
it has yet to enter into force,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.850
but states

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.523
have felt obliged to honor it.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.670
States have either signed this treaty,

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.546
or they have expressed

00:02:15.546 --> 00:02:20.546
their intention not to continue testing,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.073
at least for now.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.023
Amazing achievements.

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:29.070
How did they happen?

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:36.310
Well, some would argue that this record,

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:38.913
unheralded record of accomplishment,

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.050
happened because of deterrence.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.053
Deterrence prevented
weapons from being used,

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:49.083
on battlefields.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:54.500
Well, there's something to that argument,

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.793
but deterrence is all about threat,

00:02:58.730 --> 00:02:59.940
and the greater the threat

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.333
the greater the deterrence, or at least,

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.693
so the practitioners practice.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.890
And threats, aren't conducive

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.363
to nuclear arms control.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.620
Threats, deterrent threats

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.773
haven't reduced a single
nuclear weapon, not one.

00:03:24.104 --> 00:03:28.340
And yet global stockpiles
of nuclear weapons

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:33.313
mostly consisting of
Washington and Moscow's stocks,

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:36.333
have been reduced by over 80%.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.890
So deterrence doesn't count

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:44.040
for that 85% figure.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:45.543
Something else is involved.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:54.590
So how did we go seven decades
without battlefield use?

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:58.493
Some people say, well,
what about blind dumb luck?

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.880
Maybe that's a reason,
a contributing factor

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:09.000
and I don't discount it,
but there have been so

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.003
many instances of close calls,

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.220
mostly due to accidents,

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.363
breakdowns of commanded control.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:21.840
Dozens of close calls.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.850
Can we attribute non use

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.980
and non mushroom clouds,

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:32.980
to plain dumb luck, in
every single instance?

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:33.963
I don't think so.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:38.293
Well then we can try divine intervention.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.653
Maybe that's a contributing factor.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.540
I happen to believe it
is a contributing factor,

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:49.920
but not everybody does.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.540
Especially the rationalists

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.640
who pulled together this field

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:01.180
of deterrence, based on

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:05.353
rational actions by rational actors.

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:09.510
So what else have we got?

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.250
What else can explain

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:15.660
this extraordinary circumstance

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.103
that we all benefit from?

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.163
Well, there is this thing
called Nuclear Arms Control.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.610
And it has been

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:32.610
the product of decades
of hard diplomatic labor.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.930
And a building block approach,

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:43.493
if you like the metaphor of construction.

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:49.430
I prefer the metaphor of a safety net,

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:51.393
a nuclear safety net.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:55.550
Which has been woven assiduously

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:01.303
to stop nuclear testing,

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.683
to reduce nuclear arms,

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.403
to engage cooperatively,

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.380
to reduce nuclear danger,

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:17.120
even after the Soviet Union dissolved

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:20.093
with 39,000 weapons.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:27.313
The cooperative practices
that maintained control,

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:32.780
over this enormous nuclear stockpile,

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:36.595
some of which was outside the confines

00:06:36.595 --> 00:06:38.430
of the Russian Federation

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:42.670
when the Soviet Union dissolved.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:45.623
That was the result of hard work,

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:48.263
and the antecedents,

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:53.773
allowed for success after
the Soviet Union dissolved.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.310
So this amazing process

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:02.980
of weaving a nuclear safety net

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:07.523
the thickest strands of this net,

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:09.433
where treaties.

00:07:10.755 --> 00:07:11.588
Treaties.

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:19.083
And now these thick strands are being cut.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.913
The nuclear safety net
that we have relied on,

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.023
that has been so beneficial,

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.763
and so unheralded,

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:38.143
is being dismantled.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:47.977
The, one of the major preconditions

00:07:49.405 --> 00:07:51.903
for the steep reductions that occurred,

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:56.763
in these bloated superpower arsenals,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.600
was a treaty that banned
nationwide missile defenses.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:09.933
This treaty was negotiated
during the Nixon administration.

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:13.600
And it backstopped the deep cuts.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:14.623
It came later.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.883
But the treaty has been,

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:20.693
cut.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:26.610
This thick strand of
the nuclear safety net,

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:30.233
was cut during the George
W. Bush administration.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:38.760
As a result, Vladimir Putin

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.343
did some cutting of his own.

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:45.480
And he cut a treaty,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.560
strand, thick strand,

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:53.260
that said thou shalt not
put more than one warhead

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:56.563
on top of the missiles
we worry the most about,

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.120
intercontinental ballistic missiles.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.710
This had been an objective

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:06.650
of arms controllers for decades.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:11.650
Finally achieved by George H. W. Bush.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:17.820
Putin said, look if you guys

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:18.653
are gonna cut the ABM Treaty,

00:09:18.653 --> 00:09:23.413
I'm gonna, I'm not gonna to
abide by this obligation.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:27.740
And I'm gonna to go ahead and
put more than one missile,

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:31.840
excuse me, more than one
warhead atop of these missiles,

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:32.893
and he's doing that.

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:35.403
They're being deployed now.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:41.313
The treaty, the thick
strand in this safety net,

00:09:43.673 --> 00:09:46.670
that foreclosed the deployment

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:51.003
of missiles in Europe,

00:09:51.918 --> 00:09:55.773
or that could be directed at
Europe from certain distances.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.773
This intermediate nuclear forces treaty,

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:04.763
signed by Reagan and Gorbachev.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:09.690
A treaty that did extreme violence

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.513
to nuclear orthodoxy.

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:19.360
A treaty that broke the back
of the nuclear arms race

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.543
in 1987, cut.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:28.230
This was a combined effort,
a joint effort I would say

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:29.770
by Putin

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:34.770
and President Trump and his
national security advisor,

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:37.073
John Bolton.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:42.653
Either way, I'm throwing
out Yale alumni here,

00:10:44.843 --> 00:10:46.060
but

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.563
snip by snip by snip,

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:55.000
this nuclear safety net

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.710
is being cut.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:02.370
It now has very little
load bearing capacity.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:03.343
We don't know,

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:06.170
how little

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:07.190
until

00:11:08.983 --> 00:11:09.973
we fall through it,

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:14.173
and witness a mushroom cloud.

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:16.333
What do we do about it?

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:20.190
Well, in 1960,

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:23.773
some very very wise people,

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:26.053
came together,

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:30.853
mostly along the banks
of the Charles River,

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:33.363
but also elsewhere.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:38.223
And they asked themselves
two fundamental questions.

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:40.463
What do we want?

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.023
And what do we call it?

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:48.113
And their answers were
the same, in both cases.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:51.890
They devise this conceptualized

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:54.833
this practice of arms control.

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:59.050
Before they did this,

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:03.480
people were still
expressing fealty to general

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:04.943
and complete disarmament,

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:08.920
but they really didn't mean it.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:10.853
And they had no way of getting there.

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:17.422
Arms control, the
practice of arms control,

00:12:17.422 --> 00:12:22.160
was conceived at the right
time, in the right place

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:26.980
for a newly elected
President who were friends

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:29.003
with some of the conceptualizers,

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:32.523
and brought them into his administration,

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:35.723
where they began,

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:42.010
this amazing construction
project or weaving project.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:45.853
Well,

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:52.617
the practice of arms control seems to have

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:56.160
sputtered,

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:58.230
and it may have run its course.

00:12:58.230 --> 00:12:59.650
I believe there's plenty of room

00:12:59.650 --> 00:13:04.253
for another arms reduction
treaty with Moscow.

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:09.300
But sooner or later we're
going to have to conceive

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:11.890
of something to replace

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:16.930
our old fashioned concept of arms control.

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:18.620
2020,

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:21.623
is the new 1960.

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:27.520
So we have two central questions.

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:30.690
The same central questions.

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:34.533
What do we want and what do we call it?

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:41.293
So I will end with where I started.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:45.990
Arms control is a global service

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:52.303
designed to protect the global commons.

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:56.470
Whatever we call it

00:13:56.470 --> 00:14:01.153
in the future, to protect
the global commons,

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:04.943
we have to prevent mushroom clouds.

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:09.653
No use.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:13.193
No use.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:16.163
No battlefield use,

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:19.360
no threats to use,

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:21.520
no nuclear tests

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:24.750
as a demonstration

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:29.463
of power and evil intention,

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:31.233
to use.

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:34.875
I think that's the heart of it.

00:14:34.875 --> 00:14:37.083
But we need to think about this some more,

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:42.053
and we need a lot of help
in thinking this through.

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:43.973
Thank you.

00:14:46.377 --> 00:14:51.377
(calm music)

